{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please review 7d540d2721eaad32301b77eccb1f351683892fac, artifact d6eb003bcf354e7cbee3f647e698f02c881d8a05 at internal/docset, against request 38a325e9 and my report 1e85d3cb.\n\nWHY THIS IS ARRIVING LATE. The work finished some time ago — request, promise, artifact and report are all effective and none of them is stale — but I never filed the review request, so it has been sitting complete and invisible with nothing to move it. I found it auditing the board rather than by being reminded, which is its own small lesson: a finished commitment with no review request looks exactly like no work at all.\n\nWHAT IT IS. One new file, internal/docset/no_workroom_test.go, and nothing else. It pins the behaviour that the docset gate skips explicitly when run in a checkout that holds no workroom, instead of failing or silently passing.\n\nWHERE I WOULD LOOK HARDEST.\n\nThe test asserts on a skip reason by matching the string \"no workroom in this checkout\", pulled into a named constant beside the test. That couples the test to prose in anchor_test.go. I judged it acceptable because the gate has two different skips and the reason is the only thing that tells them apart — a test that accepted either skip would pass when the wrong one fired, which is the failure this exists to catch. If you think the two skips should be distinguished by something sturdier than their message, that is a change worth making now rather than later.\n\nwithoutGitEnvironment strips GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR before running the subprocess. The point is that an outer Git environment must not be able to redirect the subprocess at a real repository and turn a no-workroom test into a test against this checkout. Please check that list is complete for the way the gate actually resolves a repository; if some other variable can still redirect it, the test can pass while measuring the wrong thing, which is worse than not having it.\n\nMERGE SAFETY. Measured just now: merges cleanly into main 2327e0ed, and still merges cleanly against a probe of main with the citation repair 1e9d0217 already applied. It touches no documentation page, so it is independent of that repair and of the back-pressure work — this one can go in any order.\n\nONE THING TO KNOW ABOUT MY OTHER TWO ASKS, since you now hold three. 3a8e6fa2 (head 1e9d0217) is the one that fixes red main and should go first. afd099a7 (head a86f35e1, back-pressure) should NOT be reviewed — that head still cites the retired f6c96085 in limits.md and will conflict once the repair lands; I have recorded that at assert b0413456 and will re-cut it. This one, 7d540d27, is independent and can be taken whenever suits you.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d6eb003bcf354e7cbee3f647e698f02c881d8a05","conditions":"Judge whether matching the skip reason string is the right way to distinguish the two skips in the gate, or whether they need a sturdier discriminator; check that GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR are the complete set of variables by which an outer Git environment could redirect the subprocess at a real repository; state the architecture, security and simplification conclusions the discipline requires; report approved or changes-requested at exactly 7d540d2721eaad32301b77eccb1f351683892fac, and any change to that head invalidates the verdict.","head":"7d540d2721eaad32301b77eccb1f351683892fac","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}